WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.640
THE STATE OF NINTENDO:
Nintento is likely the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:08.040
most classic and well known game company in the&nbsp;
entire world. They’ve made nostalgic fun games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:13.720
that have captivated hundreds of millions of&nbsp;
people across multiple generations. The Switch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:18.200
alone has sold well over a hundred million&nbsp;
units, and they’ve even had a movie come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:24.720
out about one of their biggest IPs: Mario. But,&nbsp;
behind all these layers, the sad reality is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:30.480
Nintento has actually been undergoing a massive&nbsp;
recession. Now, it’s not like they’re about to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:37.280
bankrupt or something but they have been losing&nbsp;
ground for over 15 years now. Way back in 2009,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:43.040
for example, Nintendo was pulling in over&nbsp;
$17 billion in revenue. Over the next decade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:49.880
this would drop nearly 80% all the way down to&nbsp;
$3.6 billion. Since then, they have been staging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:55.880
a pretty strong recovery with the Switch and&nbsp;
their mobile games, but even with that, Nintendo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.880 --> 00:01:01.200
has only been able recover to a little over $10&nbsp;
billion in revenue and that’s not even accounting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:07.040
for inflation. If we throw inflation into the mix,&nbsp;
we’ll see that Nintendo is only pulling in about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:13.440
half of what they were back in 2009. And that’s&nbsp;
just the revenue side of things. If we look at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:19.720
stock side of things, we’ll see that Nintendo is&nbsp;
struggling to surpass their 2007 high and again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:24.720
that’s not even accounting for inflation. And&nbsp;
what’s worse is that to make the Switch work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:30.360
Nintendo had to basically cannablize&nbsp;
their entire business and bet it all. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:36.600
now they’re less diversified and more volatile&nbsp;
than ever. And this becomes especially dangerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.960
given that we’re approaching the end of the&nbsp;
Switch’s lifecycle with it already being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:47.040
out for nearly 7 years. Given that the Switch is&nbsp;
still selling quite well, it seems that Nintendo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:53.280
is willing to keep it around for an extra year&nbsp;
or two. But sooner or later, Nintendo will have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.280 --> 00:02:00.200
release a new console which as we’ve seen in the&nbsp;
past is often hit or miss. So, join me as we take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:15.864
a closer look at what happened to Nintendo and&nbsp;
their struggle to recapture their former glory.

00:02:15.864 --> 00:02:17.080
THE FALL BEGINS:
Taking a look back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:23.880
we can’t talk about the legacy and success of&nbsp;
Nintendo without mentioning the NES and SNES.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:29.200
Compared to modern console sales, these systems&nbsp;
didn’t actually sell all that well coming at 61&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:34.960
million units and 49 million units respectively.&nbsp;
But, their impact on the gaming industry was much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:40.960
larger than just these numbers. These consoles&nbsp;
are what really put Nintendo on the global map,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:46.520
introducing franchises like Mario and Zelda to&nbsp;
the entire world. Nintendo had a much different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:51.480
approach back then as well. It was all about&nbsp;
pushing the boundaries in terms of power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:57.160
performance, and innovation. The Nintendo 64, for&nbsp;
example, basically redefined the entire gaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:03.120
industry with its revolutionary experimentation&nbsp;
with 3D graphics. Fun fact, Nintendo was even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:09.240
experimenting with VR way back in 1995 with the&nbsp;
launch of the Nintendo Virtual Boy. And all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:14.560
that was just the console side of Nintendo.&nbsp;
Nintendo was also making substantial progress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:20.800
on the handheld side of things with the Gameboy&nbsp;
and later the DS. But, moving into the 2000s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:25.720
Nintendo would flip all of this on its head.&nbsp;
Instead of continuing to push the boundaries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:31.280
with performance and graphics, Nintendo would&nbsp;
decide to experiment with their first gimmick:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:37.160
motion controls. It turns out that motion controls&nbsp;
are actually an extremely heated topic and much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:42.520
of the core gaming community hates then with a&nbsp;
passion, and when you hear them out, it’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:48.000
surprising why. For starters, motion controls&nbsp;
are inherently less reliable than buttons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:53.360
and this makes a big difference when it comes to&nbsp;
more intense games. And second of all, people play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:59.240
video games to relax and chill out, not stand up&nbsp;
and move all over the place. But while the Wii and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:04.400
it’s motion controls didn’t appeal to hardcore&nbsp;
gamers per se, you know who it did appeal to?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:10.000
Basically everyone else and I don’t mean that in&nbsp;
a metaphorical sense. This is probably the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:14.960
console in history that grandparents bought&nbsp;
not as a Christmas present but as a console&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:20.960
for themselves. The idea was that games like Wii&nbsp;
Fit and Dance Dance Revolution were a fun way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:26.520
stay fit and active for any age group. And as&nbsp;
for younger kids, well, being able to swing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:32.040
Wii remote as a golf club or a baseball bat or a&nbsp;
tennis racket was a lot cooler than just pressing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:38.000
buttons. And all of this led to the Wii becoming&nbsp;
Nintendo’s best selling home console of all time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:43.600
selling just over 100 million units. Motion&nbsp;
controls weren’t the only gimmick that Nintendo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:49.040
was experimenting with though. After producing&nbsp;
single screen handheld devices for over a decade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:54.800
for some reason, Nintendo would decide to make the&nbsp;
DS a dual screen system. This functionality was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.800 --> 00:05:01.160
basically never useful in any game but that didn’t&nbsp;
really matter because it gave the DS a cool factor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:07.520
that helped it become the most successful handheld&nbsp;
of all time, selling over 150 million units. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:14.440
concurrent explosion of the Wii and DS made the&nbsp;
late 2000s a magical time period for Nintendo but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:20.040
it also led to a rather faulty line of reasoning.&nbsp;
The idea that Nintendo needed some sort of gimmick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:25.960
to maximize sales and reach as many people as&nbsp;
possible. The reality was that this was never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:31.640
what made Nintendo successful in the first place,&nbsp;
but nonetheless, Nintendo would end up doubling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:44.781
down on gimmicks and they would find out the&nbsp;
hard way that this wouldn’t always end well.

00:05:44.781 --> 00:05:48.040
GIMMICKS FALL FLAT:
Nintendo’s next big gimmick was of course 3D with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:55.200
the 3DS. Around the time that the 3DS launched, 3D&nbsp;
TVs were gaining a bunch of steam due to Avatar.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.200 --> 00:06:01.800
Avatar had shown that 3D could be done a tasteful&nbsp;
cinematic way that enhanced the user experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:07.440
and given that Avatar would go on to be the&nbsp;
biggest box office hit of all time, it’s no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:13.680
wonder why TV makers and eventually Nintendo also&nbsp;
wanted a piece of the pie. But as you would guess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:19.240
their implementation of 3D was nothing like&nbsp;
Avatar’s implementation. Nintendo’s implementation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:25.640
of 3D wasn’t an artistic choice, it was an eye&nbsp;
catching feature to sell consoles which it did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:32.640
somewhat do. The 3DS only sold about half as well&nbsp;
as the DS but that still added up to a solid 75&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:39.320
million units. The same, however, could not be&nbsp;
said about the Wii U. The Wii U’s gimmick was that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:45.320
you could play console games on a handheld device.&nbsp;
The catch was that you could only be like 15 feet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:51.000
away from the console so it wasn’t actually all&nbsp;
that useful, but then again, that never stopped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:58.400
the DS, Wii, or 3DS. Something that did stop the&nbsp;
Wii U though was terrible marketing. For starters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:03.760
they decided to name the console the Wii U and&nbsp;
the design looked almost identical to a Wii,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:08.560
atleast to the average person. The main controller&nbsp;
that you were supposed to use to control the Wii U&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:14.440
other than the GamePad was also just regular old&nbsp;
Wii remotes. So, it was natural that many people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:20.320
thought that the Wii U was just a pro version of&nbsp;
the Wii as opposed to a next gen console, leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:26.360
to really poor sales amongst casual buyers. As for&nbsp;
the core gaming community, these guys knew that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:33.160
the Wii U was a new console but it didn’t appeal&nbsp;
them all that much. Again, it seemed like Nintendo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:37.720
was just putting all of their eggs in the gimmick&nbsp;
basket instead of really pushing forward the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:43.400
industry in terms of perfomance. So, much of the&nbsp;
core gaming audience preferred to just get an Xbox&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:51.080
One or a PS4. And all of this was only made worse&nbsp;
by positive feedback loops. You see, the better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:56.200
a console sells, the more game developers that&nbsp;
get on board, and the more games that come out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.200 --> 00:08:02.440
the better the console sells. However, this works&nbsp;
the other way too. The worse a console sells,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:06.920
the fewer game developers that get on board,&nbsp;
and the fewer games that come out, the worse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:13.600
the console sells eventually leading to a deep&nbsp;
dark abyss in terms of sales which is precisely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:19.760
what happened to the Wii U. The Wii U would end up&nbsp;
being Nintendo’s worst selling console of all time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:27.520
only selling 13.5 million units. For perspective,&nbsp;
that’s only about an 1/8th of the Wii. Honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:33.240
this was quite a shame because despite the Wii&nbsp;
U’s flaws and shortcomings, it was actually a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:38.320
super underrated console with a bunch of solid&nbsp;
first party games like Super Mario 3D World,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:43.800
Mario Kart 8, and Splatoon. In fact, a lot of&nbsp;
these games would just get ported to the Switch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:49.360
and end up selling really well. So, the Wii U&nbsp;
definitely had a some hidden gems but in terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:55.640
of sales and financials, it was nothing but a&nbsp;
failure. And given that the 3DS and 2DS were also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.640 --> 00:09:10.105
starting to wind down in terms sales, Nintendo&nbsp;
needed a new hero product: enter the Switch.

00:09:10.105 --> 00:09:12.640
BETTING IT ALL:
By the late 2010s, Nintendo’s financials were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:19.560
looking more depressing than ever with revenue&nbsp;
being down nearly 80%. And that’s why in 2017,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:25.800
Nintendo would make an unprecedented move. The&nbsp;
move to combine their waning handheld business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:31.200
with their waning console business. The idea was&nbsp;
obviously that these businesses might be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:37.240
accomplish more together but it also meant that&nbsp;
Nintendo was risking it all on just one console:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:43.600
the Switch. To be honest, the Switch didn’t solve&nbsp;
any fundamental issue with the Wii U. Being a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:49.640
hybrid console, it was not even comparable to the&nbsp;
Playstation or Xbox in terms of performance. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:55.360
Nintendo was still very much betting on a gimmick&nbsp;
to sell the Switch. The gimmick that you could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.360 --> 00:10:00.960
take the Switch on the go. In that sense, the&nbsp;
Switch was really just a better version of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:07.720
Wii U. It even had a bunch of the same games but&nbsp;
where the Switch really made a difference was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.720 --> 00:10:13.320
marketing. The name of the console itself was&nbsp;
indicative of what the console was all about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:18.760
It was about switching between handheld mode and&nbsp;
console mode. The Switch also had new controllers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:24.840
a new console design, and a brand new generation&nbsp;
of kids to market to. And it turns out that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:29.640
face lift is all the Wii U needed in order to&nbsp;
go from being Nintendo’s worst selling console&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:35.440
to being almost their best selling console. The&nbsp;
Switch currently stands at a 132 million units&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:40.880
sold meaning that they may even be able to take&nbsp;
the crown for the best selling console of all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:47.280
time, but that title is rather misleading because&nbsp;
the Switch isn’t directly comparable to any other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:54.800
console on that list because it’s a handheld and&nbsp;
a console. The Wii and the DS sold a combined 255&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.800 --> 00:11:00.440
million units, so from that perspective, the&nbsp;
Switch is only doing about half as well and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.440 --> 00:11:06.080
that’s why Nintendo’s financials are also only&nbsp;
doing half as well. They basically cashed in on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:11.600
both their handheld audience and their console&nbsp;
audience to make the Switch a success. Maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:15.800
that’s not such a bad thing though. Thanks to&nbsp;
the rise of smartphones, selling a separate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:21.160
handheld gaming device is harder than ever. So,&nbsp;
combining handheld and console and developing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:26.280
games separately for smartphones may have actually&nbsp;
been a good long term move from Nintendo. Speaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:31.760
of mobile games, while Nintendo’s mobile games&nbsp;
are extremely popular clocking in at 738 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:37.320
total downloads, it seems that their mobile&nbsp;
revenue has been on a decline for 4 years now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:42.080
not even benefitting from pandemic. And given&nbsp;
that Nintendo themselves have admitted that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:46.680
their mobile games aren’t meant to be massive&nbsp;
revenue drivers, it doesn’t seem like mobile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:52.600
gaming is the new era Nintendo either. All of&nbsp;
this makes it very unlikely that Nintendo will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.600 --> 00:11:58.760
ever revisit that peak that they set way back&nbsp;
in the late 2000s, but maybe that’s not such a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.760 --> 00:12:04.200
bad thing. While revenue isn’t doing better than&nbsp;
ever, Nintendo has figured out how to make similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:11.880
levels of profit by doubling their margins from&nbsp;
17% to 35%. So while they may not be as ubiquitous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:17.440
as they once were, they are as profitable as&nbsp;
they once were. Whether this success continues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:23.000
on into the future is of course very dependent&nbsp;
on how their next console performs. But seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:28.960
Nintendo experiment with new avenues like mobile&nbsp;
gaming and even movies, it seems that Nintendo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:35.560
is open to adapting and evolving but only time&nbsp;
will tell. With historically high interest rates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

